      ":question/id": "1f02ee2a-1487-5adc-ad30-bf559dca8927",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-159592",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{y},$ then $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={x}^{y},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)={x}^{y+h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right).\n$$"
